---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ConverterHero from '../components/ConverterHero.astro';
import FAQAccordion from '../components/FAQAccordion.astro';
import RelatedPairs from '../components/RelatedPairs.astro';
import AmountTable from '../components/AmountTable.astro';

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL('https://usdtoworld.com');
const pairFaqs = [
  {
    q: 'How much is 1000 Singapore dollars in USD?',
    a: "It depends on the day's rate — check the converter above for the exact current figure. As a rough reference, 1000 Singapore dollars has recently converted to around 770 US dollars; the table further down this page has more common amounts already worked out.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How much is 1000 Swiss francs in USD?',
    a: "It depends on the day's rate — use the converter above for the precise figure. As a rough reference, 1000 Swiss francs has recently converted to around 1,230 US dollars; the franc has historically traded close to, and sometimes above, parity with the dollar, unlike most currencies on this site.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are SGD and CHF often grouped together?',
    a: "Not because they're related economically — Singapore and Switzerland are very different economies — but because both currencies share a reputation as unusually stable, well-managed \"safe\" currencies backed by large foreign reserves and cautious, conservative central banks. Investors and travelers often think of them the same way: as currencies less likely to see dramatic swings than an emerging-market pair.",
  },
];

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: site.toString() },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'USD to SGD & CHF', item: new URL('/usd-to-sgd', site).toString() },
  ],
};
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: pairFaqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
};
---
<Layout
  title="USD to SGD & CHF Converter — Singapore Dollar, Swiss Franc | UsdtoWorld"
  description="Convert US Dollars to Singapore Dollars or Swiss Francs with a live exchange rate. See USD to SGD, USD to CHF, and common amounts for both — two of the world's most stable currencies."
  path="/usd-to-sgd"
  schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema]}
  speakableSelectors={['h1', '.hero-sub']}
>
  <div class="container page-hero">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'USD to SGD & CHF', href: '/usd-to-sgd' }]} />
  </div>

  <ConverterHero
    title="USD to SGD Converter (Live Exchange Rate)"
    subtitle="Convert US Dollars to Singapore Dollars instantly with a live market rate. Need Swiss Francs instead? Switch the 'To' currency below, or jump to the CHF section further down this page."
    defaultFrom="USD"
    defaultTo="SGD"
  />

  <div class="container">
    <AmountTable
      fromCode="USD"
      toCode="SGD"
      fromSymbol="$"
      toSymbol="S$"
      rate={1.28}
      amounts={[10, 100, 500, 1000, 5000]}
      asOfDate="2026-08-14"
    />
  </div>

  <section class="section editorial">
    <div class="container editorial-body prose">
      <p class="editorial-byline">Reviewed by the <a href="/about#who-writes-and-reviews-this">UsdtoWorld editorial team</a> · Sourced from live market rate data</p>

      <h2 id="sgd">The Singapore dollar: managed by a currency band, not an interest rate</h2>
      <p>
        Most central banks — the Fed, the ECB, the Bank of England — set an interest rate as their main policy
        tool and let the currency float on top of it. The Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) does something
        different: it manages the Singapore dollar directly, letting it trade within an undisclosed band
        against a basket of Singapore's major trading partners' currencies, adjusting the slope and width of
        that band as needed. Interest rates in Singapore are more of a side effect of this policy than the
        policy itself — a genuinely unusual setup among the world's major currencies.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice, this makes the Singapore dollar one of the steadier currencies to convert against, since
        MAS actively manages against sharp swings rather than reacting after the fact. Singapore's role as a
        major Asian financial and trade hub, combined with large foreign reserves, reinforces that stability.
      </p>

      <h2 id="chf">The Swiss franc: the classic "safe haven" currency</h2>
      <p>
        The Swiss franc has a long-standing reputation as one of the world's safe-haven currencies — a place
        investors move money to during global uncertainty, similar to how the US dollar itself often behaves.
        That reputation rests on Switzerland's political neutrality, a conservative, independent central bank
        (the Swiss National Bank), and decades of low inflation. It's common enough that the franc actually
        strengthens during global market stress — the opposite of what happens to many other currencies — that
        the pattern has its own informal name among traders: "flight to franc."
      </p>
      <p>
        One quirk worth knowing: the Swiss National Bank held interest rates below zero for years — a policy
        called negative interest rates — specifically to discourage excessive demand for francs from pushing
        the currency too strong for Swiss exporters to compete internationally. That's a very different
        problem from what most central banks manage, and it says something about just how much steady demand
        there is for francs as a safe asset.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes with either currency</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Assuming "stable" means "identical every day":</strong> both currencies still move, just typically with less drama than an emerging-market pair — check the live converter rather than a memorized figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Underestimating card and ATM margins:</strong> a stable underlying rate doesn't mean a stable fee — banks and card networks still apply their own markup on both SGD and CHF conversions.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixing up Singapore dollars with other regional currencies:</strong> Singapore dollars are sometimes confused with Brunei dollars (the two are actually pegged 1:1 to each other, but are still separate currencies) — worth double-checking which one you actually need.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQAccordion
    faqs={pairFaqs}
    id="usd-sgd-chf-faq"
    eyebrow="USD/SGD & USD/CHF FAQ"
    heading="Common USD to SGD and CHF questions"
    description="Quick answers before you convert."
  />

  <RelatedPairs
    links={[
      { label: 'USD to HKD', href: '/usd-to-hkd' },
      { label: 'USD to JPY', href: '/usd-to-jpy' },
      { label: 'USD to EUR', href: '/usd-to-eur' },
      { label: 'USD to GBP', href: '/usd-to-gbp' },
    ]}
  />

  <script>
    import '../scripts/converter.js';
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  .editorial-body { max-width: 780px; }
</style>
